

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Both, shared module — noted. That means the logger needs to work in a CommonJS Node context ([38;5;153mrequire[39m) and in a plain
  [38;5;153m<script>[39m browser context, which constrains how it's packaged. I'll come back to that in the design.

  Next fork, and it's the one with the longest shadow:

  "Debug production issues" in a browser means the logs are on someone else's machine, so the real question is whether
  they ever reach you. Console-only logging costs nothing and changes nothing about your deployment, but you only see a
  log if a user opens devtools and reads it back to you — it doesn't actually solve production debugging, it just makes
  the local story better. Shipping logs to a remote collector is the thing that genuinely answers "what happened for
  that user," but it introduces a network dependency, an endpoint you have to run or pay for, a batching/failure story,
  and a privacy surface — this login form handles passwords, so anything shipped needs hard redaction rules. In-memory
  buffer with manual export is the middle path: logs accumulate in the page, and a user hitting an error can hand you a
  dump; no endpoint to run, no data leaves without the user acting.

  Remote collection is the expensive one to change your mind about later — it touches deployment, config, and privacy
  review. Console-only and the buffer are both cheap to upgrade from.

  My recommendation is the in-memory buffer with export, because it gets you real production evidence without standing
  up infrastructure, and it's a clean stepping stone to remote shipping if you later decide you need it.
